// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(5) forbids
// `consteval` only on "a destructor, allocation function, or deallocation
// function". A conversion function is none of those, so it may be declared
// `consteval`; §9.1(1)/(2) and front-matter §1(2) adopt [dcl.constexpr]/1-2
// and [class.conv.fct] unchanged, and those permit it too.
// §9.1(4) then requires the conversion -- an *implicit* invocation of the
// immediate function ([expr.const.imm]/1 counts implicit invocations) -- to
// produce a constant expression. The receiver is a named `constexpr` object,
// so it does.
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support the call.
//
// Call position: block scope, invoked implicitly by copy-initialization.
// Callee: `consteval` conversion function. Receiver: named object.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS -- but not because conversion
// functions are missing: scpp resolves this one to
// `Wrapped_operator_convert_int` and then reports "error: internal error: no
// generated code for resolved function 'Wrapped_operator_convert_int' called
// here", i.e. the implicit immediate invocation is lowered as a runtime call
// instead of being folded. The expectation above comes from the spec and from
// clang; do not weaken it to match the implementation.
class Wrapped {
public:
    virtual ~Wrapped() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    constexpr Wrapped(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval operator int() const {
        return this->value * 2;
    }
};

int main() {
    constexpr Wrapped w{21};
    int folded = w;
    return folded;
}
